{"ts":"2026-08-13T21:22:00.778Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_instance":"2a00cb64a3a7c61d1591525b","agent_provenance":{"model":null,"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"implementer","source":"argv"},"topic":null},"op":"create","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/id","value":"pm-brief-h7fg"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/title","value":"The pm CLI was a runtime dependency with no peer declaration, so consumers could resolve a second copy"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/description","value":"This package declared @unbrained/pm-cli in dependencies and declared no peer range at all. It is a pure extension with no bin entry: the host CLI loads it, so the host copy is the one that should serve its SDK imports. As a plain dependency npm is free to install a second copy nested under this package whenever the consumer host pin falls outside the declared range, and with no peer declaration npm had no signal that the host copy was meant to satisfy it. Sixteen sibling packages import the same SDK at runtime while declaring the CLI only as a peer plus dev dependency, so peer-only is the established and working convention. Skew matters because consecutive CLI releases disagree about whether identical history bytes are fatal, a warning, or invisible, so which copy loads can decide a workspace gate verdict."},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/type","value":"Issue"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/status","value":"open"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/priority","value":1},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/tags","value":["correctness","packaging"]},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/created_at","value":"2026-08-13T21:22:00.778Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-08-13T21:22:00.778Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/author","value":"harness:claude-code"}],"before_hash":"3cc22dff72be7b14824654a7a64ea62b04799939b2fee54c1b5f52ca60bf6df0","after_hash":"e2cd9d0c6712dac53b026cd007a5dfefbd